  "hero.title": "How Redis Answers in Microseconds",
  "hero.kicker": "Computing · Software principles · A hands-on long essay",
  "hero.sub": "One `SET user:42 \"alice\"` or `ZRANGEBYSCORE` enters the socket,<br>and a single lock-free loop answers hundreds of thousands of times a second in microseconds—so where can it stall?",
  "hero.lead": "While most databases spin up pools of worker threads, coordinate mutexes, and flush pages to disk, Redis keeps its data in RAM and runs commands on <strong>one main thread</strong>—yet routinely serves hundreds of thousands of operations per second in sub-millisecond time. This essay follows one question all the way down: <strong>the instant you send `SET user:42 \"alice\"` or `ZRANGEBYSCORE`, what happens inside Redis 8.0 from the socket byte stream through memory structures down to disk snapshots—and which paths turn microseconds into milliseconds?</strong>",
  "hero.note": "All struct byte sizes, configuration defaults, and constants follow <strong>Redis 8.0</strong> (GA 1 May 2025, tag `8.0.0`) source code and specs; the frontier chapter closes with developments up to November 2026 (Redis 8.2–8.10, Valkey 9, and shared-nothing engines). Because no `redis-server` binary is installed in this build environment, every model is reimplemented cleanly in-page and checked by `tools/crosscheck.cjs` against standard RESP2/RESP3 wire framing, SipHash-2-4 vectors, CRC16-XMODEM vectors, reverse-binary `SCAN` preservation, skiplist span invariants, and Morris counter expectation tables. For hardware and OS context, see <a href=\"../../systems/how-the-os-talks-to-hardware/\">How the OS Talks to Hardware</a>, <a href=\"../../systems/how-a-nic-moves-packets/\">How a Network Card Moves Packets</a>, <a href=\"../../systems/how-virtual-memory-works/\">How Virtual Memory Works</a>, and <a href=\"../../systems/why-compute-waits-for-memory/\">Why Compute Waits for Memory</a>.",
  "ch1.title": "Why One Main Thread Beats Locks",
  "toc.ch1": "Event loop vs locks",
  "ch1.law": "A DRAM lookup takes ~100 ns, whereas acquiring contended mutexes, parking threads, and bouncing cache lines burns microseconds; in single-node RAM, deleting locks buys more speed than adding cores.",
  "ch1.p1": "Suppose you open `redis-cli` and run three commands: write a string, insert a leaderboard score, and query a score range. Every time you press Enter, Redis's core function `aeMain` repeats one tight loop: ask the OS multiplexer (`epoll` on Linux, `kqueue` on macOS/BSD) for every ready socket in one batch, run each queued command sequentially against in-memory structures, append replies to client output buffers, and flush them back to the kernel right before sleeping again (`beforeSleep`). For how `epoll` amortizes syscall crossings, see <a href=\"../../systems/how-the-os-talks-to-hardware/#ch11\">syscalls and epoll</a>; for why RAM reads sit around ~100 ns, see <a href=\"../../systems/why-compute-waits-for-memory/#ch1\">DRAM latency</a>.",
  "ch1.code.aria": "A minimal Redis command round-trip sequence",
  "ch1.code": "SET user:42 \"alice\"\n# -> +OK\\r\\n\n\nZADD rank 100 \"alice\"\n# -> :1\\r\\n\n\nZRANGEBYSCORE rank 90 150 WITHSCORES\n# -> *2\\r\\n$5\\r\\nalice\\r\\n$3\\r\\n100\\r\\n",
  "ch1.p2": "<strong>Why not let eight worker threads mutate the same hash table in parallel?</strong> Whenever two threads touch a hot key or shared hash table metadata, they need a mutex: an atomic CAS instruction to lock, an OS futex sleep and context switch on collision, plus repeated L1/L2 cache-line invalidation across cores. Keeping command execution on one thread removes every data-structure lock. The flip side is <strong>head-of-line blocking</strong>: if one command stalls for milliseconds (such as an `O(N)` `KEYS *` scan), every client behind it waits in line.",
  "dg.loop.cap": "Figure 1. One iteration of Redis's main event loop aeMain: wait for ready sockets, parse and run commands, flush replies.",
