  "ch1.say.fast": "The single lock-free loop sustains <em>{loop}</em>. With {t} threads contending at {hot}% hot keys, mutex waits and cache-line ping-pong drag locked throughput to {lock} (threads scale linearly only as contention drops near 0%).",
  "ch1.say.stall": "Normally every command finishes in microseconds, but once request #3 holds the loop for <em>{p99}</em>, all <em>{blk}</em> queued requests stall behind it and tail latency jumps 1,000×.",
  "ch1.p3": "Under the default 50% hot-key contention, the lock-free loop sustains 500 kops/s while 4 mutex-locked threads drop to 313 kops/s; injecting one 2 ms `KEYS *` stall blocks all 17 queued requests behind it for 2.00 ms. That trade-off explains both Redis's speed and its golden rule: never run O(N) scans inside the command loop.",
  "ch1.limit": "Model limits: this analytical model approximates mutex overhead as serialized critical-section fraction plus cache-line bounce; real throughput also depends on socket I/O framing (chapter 6) and OS scheduler load.",
  "ch2.title": "RESP2 / RESP3 Framing & Pipelining",
  "toc.ch2": "RESP & pipelining",
  "ch2.law": "Parsing length-prefixed wire bytes takes tenths of a microsecond, whereas a network round-trip takes ~200 µs; pipelining a batch of commands pays that wire delay once instead of N times.",
  "ch2.p1": "Clients do not talk to Redis in plain whitespace lines; they send length-prefixed frames in <strong>RESP</strong> (REdis Serialization Protocol). On the wire, `SET user:42 alice` is `*3\\r\\n$3\\r\\nSET\\r\\n$7\\r\\nuser:42\\r\\n$5\\r\\nalice\\r\\n` (37 bytes total). Why prepend `$7` and `$5`? For <strong>binary safety</strong>: even if a value contains `\\r\\n` or `\\0`, `readQueryFromClient` skips straight over exact byte counts inside `client-&gt;querybuf` to populate `argv[]`, without scanning every byte for delimiters. For how a packet traverses user space, kernel, and NIC, see <a href=\"../../systems/how-a-nic-moves-packets/#ch3\">a packet round-trip</a>.",
  "dg.resp.cap": "Figure 2. Length-prefixed RESP tokens sliced zero-copy from querybuf, and sequential waiting (red) vs pipelined batching (gold).",
  "dg.resp.aria": "Diagram: top shows SET user:42 alice RESP tokens entering querybuf; bottom compares 3 sequential round-trips against 1 pipelined round-trip",
  "dg.resp.wire": "Length-prefixed wire bytes",
  "dg.resp.qbuf": "querybuf zero-copy argv[] slice",
  "dg.resp.seq": "Sequential: 3 × (RTT + CPU)",
  "dg.resp.pip": "Pipelined: 1 × RTT + 3 × CPU",
  "ch2.p2": "Redis 8.0 speaks both <strong>RESP2</strong> (arrays `*` and bulk strings `$`, where clients must infer that `HGETALL`'s even array is a key-value map) and <strong>RESP3</strong> (negotiated via `HELLO 3`, adding explicit maps `%`, doubles `,`, booleans `#`, and null `_`). Either way, <strong>parsing is never the main latency cost—sequential round-trips are</strong>. Sending 20 commands one by one across a 200 µs LAN round-trip takes 4.02 ms; pipelining all 20 together pays one RTT plus 20 server parse-and-exec cycles, finishing in 224 µs—a 18.0× speedup.",
