  "ch3.title": "redisObject, 44-Byte `embstr`, and `listpack`",
  "toc.ch3": "Objects & encodings",
  "ch3.law": "Every value in Redis wears a 16-byte `redisObject` header; integers ride inside the pointer, strings up to 44 bytes fuse into one 64-byte `jemalloc` block (`embstr`), and small collections flatten into `listpack` arrays.",
  "ch3.p1": "On 64-bit servers, a Redis value is wrapped in a `16`-byte `struct redisObject`: 4 bits `type`, 4 bits `encoding`, 24 bits `lru` (chapter 8), 32 bits `refcount`, and an 8-byte `void *ptr`. That indirection lets one `SET` or `HSET` pick the tightest layout behind the scenes:",
  "ch3.i1": "<strong>Inline integers (`OBJ_ENCODING_INT`)</strong>: storing `\"42\"` writes the integer `42` straight into `*ptr`, eliminating any string buffer; values `0..9,999` even reuse `shared.integers[]` at startup for <strong>0 new bytes</strong> allocated.",
  "ch3.i2": "<strong>Embedded strings (`embstr`) vs `raw`</strong>: up to <strong>44 bytes</strong>, the `16`-byte `redisObject`, `3`-byte `sdshdr8` header (`len`, `alloc`, `flags`), payload, and `\\0` total `16 + 3 + 44 + 1 = 64` bytes—fitting one 64-byte `jemalloc` class and one cache line in <strong>1 `malloc`</strong>. At 45 bytes, Redis splits into `raw` encoding (<strong>2 separate `malloc` calls</strong>, `80` bytes total, plus an extra pointer hop).",
  "ch3.i3": "<strong>`listpack` replacing `ziplist`</strong>: small Hashes (default ≤ `512` entries, keys/values ≤ 64 bytes) and ZSETs (≤ 128 entries, ≤ 64 bytes) store items contiguously in a `listpack`. Old `ziplist` entries stored the <em>previous</em> element's byte length (`prevlen`), so inserting a 254+ byte item could widen `prevlen` from 1 to 5 bytes and trigger a chain-reaction <strong>cascade update</strong> across every trailing item. `listpack` fixes this by storing only each entry's <em>own</em> `backlen` at its tail, supporting backward traversal with zero cascade rewrites.",
  "dg.robj.cap": "Figure 3. String encodings (INT, 44-byte single-block embstr, two-block raw) and a listpack entry layout.",
  "dg.robj.aria": "Diagram: comparing INT pointer-packed values, 64-byte single-malloc embstr, dual-malloc raw strings, and listpack entries with self-length backlen",
