  "ch3.say.embstr": "At {len} bytes, <code>embstr</code> packs <code>redisObject</code> + <code>sdshdr8</code> + payload into <em>1 malloc ({strb})</em>; the {entries}-entry Hash takes {hb}.",
  "ch3.say.raw": "Past the 44-byte cliff, Redis falls back to <code>raw</code>: header and buffer split into <em>2 mallocs ({strb})</em> with an extra pointer dereference.",
  "ch3.p2": "A 64-entry Hash with 44-byte values takes 4,096 bytes packed in `listpack`, versus 6,200 bytes once expanded into a `hashtable` with bucket arrays and `dictEntry` pointers.",
  "ch3.limit": "Model limits: models standard 64-bit `jemalloc` size classes without counting top-level keyspace `dictEntry` pointers or Redis 8.2's unified `kvobj` (covered in chapter 10).",
  "ch4.title": "The `dict` Hash Table & Progressive Rehashing",
  "toc.ch4": "dict & progressive rehash",
  "ch4.law": "Resizing a ten-million-key hash table in one blocking loop would freeze clients for hundreds of milliseconds; `dict` keeps two tables (`ht[0]` and `ht[1]`) and migrates one bucket per command.",
  "ch4.p1": "Every key lookup (`GET`, `SET`) and large Hash field lookup hits `src/dict.c`. Keys are hashed with <strong>SipHash-1-2</strong> (1 compression round, 2 finalization rounds, initialized with a random per-process seed against hash-flooding DoS) into buckets starting at size `4`. Redis even uses pointer bit-tagging on 8-byte aligned addresses (`ENTRY_PTR_MASK`) and omits value pointers on pure sets (`dictEntryNoValue`).",
  "ch4.p2": "When load hits 1:1 (deferred to `5`:1 while a `fork()` child runs so mutations do not copy excess CoW pages, chapter 7), `dict` allocates a double-sized `ht[1]`. Rather than freezing the loop to rehash millions of entries at once, Redis sets `rehashidx = 0` and moves <strong>1 non-empty bucket on every incoming command</strong> (`_dictRehashStep`), plus 1 ms background slices (`dictRehashMilliseconds`) when idle. Lookups check `ht[0]` then `ht[1]`; new inserts go straight to `ht[1]`, keeping tail latency flat in microseconds.",
  "dg.rehash.cap": "Figure 4. Progressive rehashing: reads check both tables, writes go to ht[1], and each command advances rehashidx by one bucket.",
  "dg.rehash.aria": "Diagram: ht[0] buckets on the left migrating via rehashidx into double-size ht[1] buckets on the right",
