  "ch5.title": "Sorted Sets (`ZSET`) & the Skiplist",
  "toc.ch5": "ZSET & skiplist",
  "ch5.law": "A `ZSET` pairs an O(1) hash table (`ZSCORE`) with a doubly-linked skiplist (`p = 1/4`, `span` counts on every forward link), achieving O(log N) range scans and exact rank arithmetic without tree rotations.",
  "ch5.p1": "Returning to our opening `ZRANGEBYSCORE rank 90 150`: a hash table alone cannot iterate by score order, while a balanced binary tree cannot look up a member's score in O(1) by name. Past its `listpack` threshold (default > 128 entries or > 64 bytes), a Redis `ZSET` runs <strong>both structures side by side</strong>, sharing pointers to the same member strings and scores:",
  "ch5.i1": "A `dict` mapping `member -&gt; score` for O(1) `ZSCORE`;",
  "ch5.i2": "A `zskiplist` (`src/t_zset.c`) ordered by `(score, member)` at level 0, with a `backward` link for reverse scans (`ZREVRANGE`). On insert, `zslRandomLevel` flips coins with probability <strong>`p = 0.25`</strong> up to <strong>`32` levels</strong>, and every forward link stores <strong>`span`</strong>: the count of level-0 nodes it jumps over.",
  "ch5.p2": "<strong>Why did antirez pick a skiplist over a red-black tree?</strong> Range scans simply walk level 0 forward pointers without stack/parent backtracking, and `ZRANK` needs no tree rotations: summing `span` along every forward step taken during search yields the exact 1-based rank in O(log N).",
  "dg.skiplist.cap": "Figure 5. Dual-indexed ZSET: companion dict for O(1) ZSCORE plus multi-level zskiplist towers carrying forward span counts.",
  "dg.skiplist.aria": "Diagram: dict hash map on top, five zskiplist nodes below with forward pointers labeled span=4 and span=2",
