  "ch7.title": "Persistence Without Stopping the World: `fork()` CoW & Multi-Part AOF",
  "toc.ch7": "Persistence & CoW",
  "ch7.law": "To write a consistent point-in-time snapshot without halting writes, Redis relies on `fork()` and OS Copy-on-Write over 4 KiB pages; the more scattered the writes during `BGSAVE`, the closer memory usage climbs toward 2×.",
  "ch7.p1": "Memory vanishes on power loss. Calling `BGSAVE` clones the Redis process via `fork()` in milliseconds so a child process can stream an RDB snapshot to disk while the parent keeps serving writes. The trick lies in virtual memory's <strong>Copy-on-Write (CoW)</strong> (detailed page-table mechanics in <a href=\"../../systems/how-virtual-memory-works/#ch9\">fork() and Copy-on-Write</a>):",
  "ch7.i1": "Right after `fork()`, parent and child share read-only mappings to the <strong>same physical 4 KiB RAM pages</strong> (`1.00×` memory);",
  "ch7.i2": "Read commands copy nothing;",
  "ch7.i3": "Modifying even <strong>1 byte</strong> inside a page triggers a minor page fault: the kernel pauses the parent briefly and copies the full 4 KiB page before updating it. Under heavy random writes, CoW can nearly <strong>double RAM consumption</strong>.",
  "ch7.p2": "For tighter durability, the <strong>Append-Only File (AOF)</strong> logs every mutation under `appendfsync`: `always` (fsync every write, lowest loss, disk-bound), `everysec` (background fsync every second, ≤ 1 s loss), or `no` (kernel decides). <strong>Multi-Part AOF</strong> (Redis 7.0 through 8.0) splits persistence into `base` (RDB/AOF preamble), `incr` files, and a `manifest`, avoiding massive single-file rewrite stalls.",
  "dg.cow.cap": "Figure 7. 4 KiB Copy-on-Write page sharing during BGSAVE (clean shared vs dirty copied) and Multi-Part AOF files.",
  "dg.cow.aria": "Diagram: parent and child sharing read-only 4 KiB pages on the left while parent writes allocate copied 4 KiB pages on the right; Multi-Part AOF files below",
  "dg.cow.par": "Parent (R/W)",
  "dg.cow.chd": "Child (RDB)",
