  "ch7.say.idle": "No writes during snapshot: parent and child share all 64 pages at <em>{ram}</em> RAM, with AOF on <code>{aof}</code> ({loss} window).",
  "ch7.say.uniform": "Uniformly scattered writes ({writes} mutations) touched and duplicated <em>{copied} / 64</em> 4 KiB pages, driving RAM to <em>{ram}</em> even if each edit touched a few bytes.",
  "ch7.say.skew": "The same {writes} writes skewed to hot keys touched only <em>{copied} / 64</em> pages, holding RAM at <em>{ram}</em>—hot workloads amortize CoW copies.",
  "ch7.p3": "At 50% write volume, uniform mutations amplify memory to 1.59×, whereas 10%-hot skewed mutations stay at 1.19×.",
  "ch7.limit": "Model limits: demonstrates page duplication on a 64-page grid; real `fork()` latency also depends on page-table size and Transparent Huge Pages (THP turns 4 KiB copies into 2 MiB latency spikes, so Redis warns against THP).",
  "ch8.title": "Expiration & Eviction: Approximate LRU & Morris LFU",
  "toc.ch8": "Expiration & eviction",
  "ch8.law": "Maintaining a global doubly-linked list over millions of keys would burn 16 bytes per key in pointers and lock mutations; Redis packs eviction clocks and probabilistic logarithmic frequencies into 24 bits via bounded sampling.",
  "ch8.p1": "When you set a `TTL`, Redis combines <strong>lazy deletion on access</strong> with periodic random sampling in `activeExpireCycle` (`src/expire.c`): per cycle, each database samples <strong>`20` TTL keys</strong>, frees expired ones, and repeats only while the sampled expired fraction stays above <strong>`10%`</strong> (at default `active-expire-effort = 1`).",
  "ch8.p2": "And when RAM hits `maxmemory`, Redis avoids a global LRU linked list—which would spend <strong>16 bytes per key</strong> just on `prev`/`next` pointers (16 MB per million keys)—by packing a <strong>24-bit `lru` field</strong> inside every `redisObject`:",
  "ch8.i1": "<strong>Approximate LRU</strong>: stores a coarse seconds clock; on eviction, Redis samples <strong>`maxmemory-samples` (default `5`)</strong> random keys into a `16`-slot `EvictionPool` and evicts the oldest candidate. In our 200-key simulation, `5` samples hit within the coldest 8.1% on average with zero linked-list pointers.",
  "ch8.i2": "<strong>8-bit Morris Counter LFU</strong>: splits the 24-bit `lru` field into a <strong>16-bit minute timestamp (`ldt`)</strong> and an <strong>8-bit counter (`0..255`)</strong> initialized at `LFU_INIT_VAL = 5`. On access, `LFULogIncr` increments by 1 only with probability `p = 1 / ((counter - 5) × lfu_log_factor + 1)`. At default factor `10`, 1,000 hits reach an expected counter of `19`, 100k hits reach `146`, and ~1M hits saturate `255`—capturing six orders of magnitude in one byte.",
  "dg.evict.cap": "Figure 8. How the same 24-bit robj.lru field switches between a 24-bit LRU clock and a 16-bit LDT + 8-bit Morris counter.",
  "dg.evict.aria": "Diagram: top shows 24-bit LRU clock in seconds; bottom splits 24 bits into 16-bit minute timestamp ldt and 8-bit Morris frequency counter",
  "dg.evict.lru": "LRU mode: full 24 bits hold seconds clock",
